{"experiments":[  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Shallow and deep strata exhibit divergent long‐term trends in fish biomass and productivity, with shallow reefs showing a stronger increase in productivity over time due to warming surface waters.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare temporal trends in annual mean biomass and productivity between shallow and deep depth strata.",  
      "steps":"1. Subset the reef data by depth stratum ('Shallow' vs 'Deep').  \n2. For each stratum and year, aggregate total biomass and total productivity (sum of 'biomass' and 'prod' columns across transects).  \n3. Compute annual mean biomass and mean productivity per stratum (divide totals by number of transects or reefs sampled).  \n4. Fit two separate linear (or GAM) models: mean_biomass ~ year and mean_prod ~ year for each stratum.  \n5. Test whether the slope for shallow productivity vs year is significantly greater than that for deep (e.g., using interaction term in a combined model: prod ~ year * depth).  \n6. Check model residuals for autocorrelation and, if necessary, use a generalized least squares model with an AR(1) structure.  \n7. Visualize the two time series with trend lines and 95% confidence intervals on a single plot.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of slope estimates and p‐values for biomass and productivity trends by depth.  \n2. Plot of annual mean biomass and productivity over time for shallow vs deep strata.  \n3. Diagnostic plots (ACF of residuals, QQ‐plots) for model validation."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Strictly protected reefs (Cabo Pulmo) exhibit lower interannual variability in total fish biomass compared to unprotected reefs, indicating greater temporal stability.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Quantify and compare the temporal stability (coefficient of variation) of annual total biomass among protection status categories.",  
      "steps":"1. Assign each transect to one of the four protection_status levels (cabo pulmo, area protegida, weak regulations, sin proteccion).  \n2. For each reef–year combination, sum biomass across transects to get annual reef‐level biomass.  \n3. For each reef across the time series, compute the coefficient of variation (CV = standard deviation / mean) of annual biomass.  \n4. Group reefs by protection_status and compare distributions of CVs across groups.  \n5. Use a Kruskal–Wallis test to test for differences in CV among protection levels, followed by pairwise Wilcoxon tests.  \n6. Visualize boxplots of CV by protection_status with significance annotations.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of median CV of biomass per protection_status and test statistics.  \n2. Boxplot of CV by protection_status.  \n3. Summary of pairwise comparisons."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Small fishes (size ≤8 cm, cryptobenthic proxies) contribute a disproportionately large share of total fish biomass production relative to their share of biomass.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare the proportion of productivity versus biomass attributable to small fishes across reefs.",  
      "steps":"1. Define 'small' fish as records with size ≤8 cm and 'large' fish as >8 cm.  \n2. For each transect (or reef–year), sum biomass and productivity separately for small and large fishes.  \n3. Compute the share of small‐fish biomass and share of small‐fish productivity (small_biomass/total_biomass; small_prod/total_prod).  \n4. Across all reef–years, compare the distributions of these two shares.  \n5. Use a paired Wilcoxon signed‐rank test to test whether share_prod_small > share_biomass_small.  \n6. Plot a two‐panel histogram or density plot of both shares, and a scatterplot of share_prod_small vs share_biomass_small with the 1:1 line.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Test result (statistic and p‐value) comparing small‐fish production vs biomass shares.  \n2. Density plots of share of biomass and share of production for small fishes.  \n3. Scatterplot with 1:1 reference line."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Reefs with higher mean chlorophyll‐a concentrations have relatively greater productivity in mid‐trophic‐level (trophic_level ≈3.0–3.9) fishes compared to lower‐trophic‐level fishes.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess the relationship between mean_chla and the ratio of mid‐level fish productivity to low‐level fish productivity.",  
      "steps":"1. Bin records into two trophic groups: low trophic (trophic_level <3.0) and mid trophic (3.0 ≤ trophic_level <4.0).  \n2. For each reef–year, sum 'prod' separately for low and mid trophic bins.  \n3. Compute the ratio R = prod_mid / prod_low for each reef–year (exclude cases where prod_low = 0).  \n4. Merge R with reef–year mean_chla.  \n5. Fit a linear model: log(R) ~ mean_chla (with log‐transform to normalize ratio).  \n6. Check residuals and, if necessary, use a robust regression.  \n7. Visualize scatterplot of log(R) vs mean_chla with regression line and confidence band.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Regression output (slope, intercept, R², p‐value).  \n2. Scatterplot of log‐ratio vs mean_chla with trend line.  \n3. Residual diagnostic plots."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"The proportion of large‐bodied fishes (size ≥50 cm) in the community declines with increasing latitude (degree), reflecting cooler‐water species being smaller in warmer low‐latitude reefs.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test for a latitudinal trend in the share of large fishes across reefs.",  
      "steps":"1. Define 'large' fish as size ≥50 cm.  \n2. For each reef (pooling all years) or reef–year, compute total number of individuals and count of large individuals.  \n3. Calculate share_large = count_large / total_count for each reef (or reef–year).  \n4. For each reef, take the long‐term average share_large and the reef’s mean 'degree'.  \n5. Fit a generalized linear model (binomial‐family with weights=total_count) of share_large ~ degree.  \n6. Test significance of the degree term.  \n7. Plot share_large vs degree with the fitted model curve and confidence band.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Model summary for effect of degree on large‐fish proportion.  \n2. Plot of large‐fish share against latitude.  \n3. Table of reef‐level summary statistics (degree, share_large, sample size). "  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Marine Protected Areas (MPAs) have lower annual community turnover (year‐to‐year Bray‐Curtis dissimilarity) than unprotected reefs, indicating greater ecological resilience.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Quantify and compare temporal turnover of species composition inside and outside MPAs.",  
      "steps":"1. Label each reef–transect with MPA status: protected (protection_status ≠ 'sin proteccion') vs unprotected.  \n2. For each reef, create a species‐by‐year abundance matrix (sum 'quantity' by species and year).  \n3. Compute year‐to‐year Bray‐Curtis dissimilarity for each reef between consecutive years.  \n4. For each reef, compute the mean annual dissimilarity.  \n5. Compare the distributions of mean turnover between protected and unprotected reefs.  \n6. Use a Wilcoxon rank‐sum test to assess differences.  \n7. Visualize density plots or boxplots of mean turnover by protection category.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Test result comparing mean turnover inside vs outside MPAs.  \n2. Boxplots of year‐to‐year turnover by protection category.  \n3. Summary statistics (mean, median, SD) of turnover for both groups."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Beta diversity among reefs within a region is driven more by species turnover than nestedness, and the relative contributions of these components change over time under warming trends.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Partition regional beta diversity into turnover and nestedness components for early vs late periods.",  
      "steps":"1. Define two time periods (e.g., 1998–2005 vs 2016–2024).  \n2. For each region and period, build a presence–absence site (reef) × species matrix (species present in any year of the period).  \n3. Use a beta‐diversity partitioning method (e.g., Baselga’s betapart package in R or equivalent python implementation) to compute total beta diversity, turnover, and nestedness components within each region and period.  \n4. For each region, compare turnover and nestedness proportions between early and late periods.  \n5. Test statistically (paired t‐test across regions) whether turnover component proportion declines and nestedness increases over time.  \n6. Visualize bar plots of turnover vs nestedness proportions by region and period.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of beta diversity components (turnover, nestedness) for each region and period.  \n2. Statistical test results comparing early vs late.  \n3. Bar charts showing component proportions by region."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Reefs with higher species richness per unit productivity (species_prod_ratio) are less impacted by warming, showing weaker declines in richness with increasing mean_sst.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Evaluate whether reefs with high baseline species‐to‐productivity ratios exhibit greater resistance to SST increases.",  
      "steps":"1. For each reef–year, compute species richness (count of unique species) and total productivity (sum of 'prod').  \n2. Calculate species_prod_ratio = richness / total_prod.  \n3. For each reef, compute its long‐term median species_prod_ratio and then assess the relationship between annual richness and mean_sst, separately for reefs above vs below median ratio.  \n4. Fit two mixed‐effects models (using reef as random effect): richness ~ mean_sst for high‐ratio reefs and low‐ratio reefs.  \n5. Compare SST slope estimates between the two groups to test if high‐ratio reefs have weaker negative SST effects.  \n6. Visualize fitted lines for both groups on richness vs mean_sst.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Mixed‐model summaries for both high and low ratio groups.  \n2. Plot of species richness vs mean_sst colored by ratio group with trend lines.  \n3. Table of slope differences and confidence intervals."  
    }  
  }  
]}
